16</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ajzen</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The theory of planned behaviour</article-title><source>Organ Behav Hum Dec</source><year>1991</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>211</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0749-5978(91)90020-T</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ogden</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Some problems with social cognition models: A pragmatic and conceptual analysis</article-title><source>Health Psychol</source><year>2003</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>424</fpage><lpage>428</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12940399</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1037/0278-6133.22.4.424</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Eccles</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Grimshaw</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Walker</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Johnston</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pitts</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Changing the behavior of healthcare professionals: the use of theory in promoting the uptake of research findings</article-title><source>J Clin Epidemiol</source><year>2005</year><volume>58</volume><fpage>107</fpage><lpage>112</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15680740</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.jclinepi.2004.09.002</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Coleman</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name><name><surname>Katz</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Menzel</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><source>Medical innovation: a diffusion study</source><year>1966</year><publisher-name>Indianapolis: Bobbs-Merrill</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rogers</surname><given-names>EM</given-names></name></person-group><source>The diffusion of innovation</source><year>1995</year><edition>4</edition><publisher-name>New York: Free Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Strauss</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Fagerhough</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Suczek</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Wiener</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><source>Social organization of medical work</source><year>1997</year><publisher-name>London: Transaction</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lehoux</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><source>The problem of health technology: policy implications for modern healthy care systems</source><year>2006</year><publisher-name>London: Routledge</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Grimshaw</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Eccles</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Campbell</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Elbourne</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cluster randomized trials of professional and organizational behavior change interventions in health care settings</article-title><source>Ann Am Acad Polit Soc Sci</source><year>2005</year><volume>599</volume><fpage>71</fpage><lpage>93</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1177/0002716205274576</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fararo</surname><given-names>TJ</given-names></name></person-group><source>The meaning of general theoretical sociology: tradition and formalization</source><year>1989</year><publisher-name>Cambridge: Cambridge University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berger</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Luckman</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><source>The Social Construction of Reality: A Treatise in the Sociology of Knowledge</source><year>1966</year><publisher-name>London: Penguin</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Flyvbjerg</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><source>Making social science matter: why social inquiry fails and how it can succeed again</source><year>2001</year><publisher-name>Cambridge: Cambridge University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Suchman</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name></person-group><source>Plans and situated actions: the problem of human machine communication</source><year>1987</year><publisher-name>Cambridge: Cambridge University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mair</surname><given-names>FS</given-names></name><name><surname>Crowley</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Bundred</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Prevalence, aetiology and management of heart failure in general practice</arti